   "tier": "participant",
   "accent": "every accent in the cast at once: Southern English carries the words; British female, the druid, Earth, the North, the syllable-timed East, the two American machines, Scotland and Received Pronunciation are placed on it",
   "gender": "all of them",
   "measured": "every interval — the octaves, the unisons and the octaves-down — is measured per render, and so is the timing: the manifest reports the largest wander each layer's delivery had against the reference's before it was locked",
   "note": "UNISON HAS NO SINGLE OWNER. Two people saying the same words together is what a choir does to a solo line: the pitch stops belonging to a person and starts belonging to the statement. That is the register the realm uses about itself, and the whole realm now says it. THE OCTAVE IS CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT, and the figure this voice was built on was wrong: 94.6 Hz against 183.8 Hz is a ratio of 1.943, which three places in the codebase called \"eleven cents flat of an octave\". It is FIFTY — 1200*log2(1.943/2) = -50.1 cents, a quarter tone — and jaimla at 183.8 against the 189.2 an octave above neural beat 5.4 times a second. `harmony` measures both f0 on the document's own opening and pulls every layer onto its stated interval; a layer whose fundamental cannot be measured, or whose correction would be more than an octave, is left on its own pitch rather than pitched by a guess, and says so by its absence from the harmony report."
  },
  "harmony": "every interval is MEASURED per render (autocorrelation f0 on the body and the layer) and reported in the render manifest as a multiplier per layer; the registry names the interval, the render proves it"
 },
 "note": "OVERLORD is fourteen voices in one delivery: neural carries the words and the rest of the cast is placed on him in bands. The f0 tracker sees two fundamentals — the body near 99 Hz and Jaimla on the octave near 195 — so p90 is the octave and 27% of frames are 'octave errors' by the tracker's own definition. That is not an error in the voice; it is the chord. Compare OVERLORD by its eight metrics, not by a single f0."
}
